WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.941
Your phone's map app picks the fastest way to a friend's birthday dinner in a second, out of hundreds of routes, without driving any of them.

2
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:15.556
It knows how long each road is, and it guesses how busy each one will be.

3
00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:20.036
Tonight it sends you past the football stadium an hour before a match.

4
00:00:20.036 --> 00:00:21.561
Forty minutes in traffic.

5
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.621
So, was the search broken?

6
00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:24.904
Hold that thought.

7
00:00:24.904 --> 00:00:29.253
A database has the same job every time you ask it a question.

8
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:36.025
Every night, a report counts chargebacks, payments that customers disputed, at shops in Amsterdam.

9
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:42.034
It joins three tables: 10,000 shops, a million payments, and 20,000 chargebacks.

10
00:00:42.034 --> 00:00:46.167
A join pairs the rows of two tables that belong together.

11
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:54.955
Before anything runs, the planner, the part of the database that picks how to run a query, has to choose which two tables to join first.

12
00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:58.379
The order changes how many rows pile up in the middle.

13
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.355
And the number of orders explodes.

14
00:01:01.355 --> 00:01:10.377
Adding one table at a time, 3 tables can be joined in 6 orders, 6 tables in 720, and 10 tables in 3.6 million.

15
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:14.657
Pricing every one works for 3 tables and is hopeless for 12.

16
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:16.365
But orders share parts.

17
00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:17.797
Think of the map again.

18
00:01:17.797 --> 00:01:24.148
The fastest way to the bridge is still the fastest way to the bridge, whatever road you take after it.

19
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:31.243
So the planner finds the best plan for each small group of tables once, and builds bigger groups only from those.

20
00:01:31.243 --> 00:01:36.379
Single tables first, then every pair that a join condition connects, then all three.

21
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:42.931
That's dynamic programming: solve each small problem once, keep the answer, and build the big one from it.

22
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:48.932
IBM's System R, one of the first SQL databases, introduced it in 1979.

23
00:01:48.932 --> 00:01:59.263
It visits about 1,000 groups for 10 tables, instead of 3.6 million orders, and PostgreSQL searches this way up to 12 tables.

24
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.237
Now each box gets a guess.

25
00:02:01.237 --> 00:02:07.449
The shops have three conditions, and each keeps a tenth, so the planner multiplies: 10 shops.

26
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:11.168
Shops with their payments: about 1,000 rows.

27
00:02:11.168 --> 00:02:13.796
Payments with chargebacks: 20,000.

28
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.721
The lab's price is the rows the joins produce along the way.

29
00:02:18.721 --> 00:02:23.443
Plan A, shops with payments first: 1,000, then 20, so 1,020.

30
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:27.738
Plan B, payments with chargebacks first: 20,020.

31
00:02:27.738 --> 00:02:29.620
Plan A wins by a mile.

32
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:30.978
Here's the catch.

33
00:02:30.978 --> 00:02:35.029
Every shop in Amsterdam is in the Netherlands and takes euros.

34
00:02:35.029 --> 00:02:38.362
So how many shops really pass the three conditions?

35
00:02:38.362 --> 00:02:39.408
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:43.714
1,000, not 10.

37
00:02:43.714 --> 00:02:47.456
The second and third conditions removed nobody.

38
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:56.015
And the error climbs: the first join makes 100,000 rows, not 1,000, and the answer is 2,000, not 20.

39
00:02:56.233 --> 00:03:03.687
Priced with the real counts, plan A does 102,000 rows of work, and plan B would have done 22,000.

40
00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:06.684
The chosen plan did 4.6 times the work.

41
00:03:06.684 --> 00:03:08.605
Well, that's the stadium.

42
00:03:08.833 --> 00:03:10.673
So, was the search broken?

43
00:03:10.673 --> 00:03:10.885
No.

44
00:03:10.885 --> 00:03:14.848
It found the cheapest plan for the numbers it was given.

45
00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:25.804
In PostgreSQL itself, the same wrong guess picked a loop that looked up the payments a thousand separate times: 103,172 pages touched.

46
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.345
Tell it that the city decides the country and the currency, with CREATE STATISTICS, and it guesses 1,000 shops.

47
00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:42.137
The same search picks plan B, reads the payments once, and touches 6,542 pages, 16 times fewer.

48
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.977
It's tempting to force the order yourself.

49
00:03:44.977 --> 00:03:46.096
Don't start there.

50
00:03:46.096 --> 00:03:48.893
When a plan is slow, check the guesses first.

51
00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:58.951
Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE, which prints each step's guessed and actual rows, read it from the bottom up, and fix the first step that's off by 10 times or more.

52
00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:03.452
Often a plain ANALYZE, which refreshes the statistics, is enough.

53
00:04:03.452 --> 00:04:09.963
In one benchmark, about 1 in 10 queries ran more than 10 times slower on PostgreSQL's own guesses.

54
00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:13.843
And here the map app is ahead: it re-routes you on the way.

55
00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:16.772
PostgreSQL never changes a plan once it starts.

56
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:21.765
So the moral of the story: the planner picks the best plan for its guesses.

57
00:04:21.765 --> 00:04:24.333
A wrong plan usually means a wrong guess.

58
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.874
One quick check.

59
00:04:25.874 --> 00:04:29.457
The shops step says 10 guessed, 1,000 actual.

60
00:04:29.457 --> 00:04:33.677
The join above it says 1,000 guessed, 100,000 actual.

61
00:04:33.677 --> 00:04:35.349
Which one do you fix?

62
00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:36.623
Pause and think.

63
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:40.573
The shops.

64
00:04:40.573 --> 00:04:44.199
The join above is only passing the same error up.
